Whenever we write $T^{\otimes n} f$ or $T \mathcal{F}$,
we view the signatures as column vectors;
similarly for $f T^{\otimes n} $ or $\mathcal{F} T$ as row vectors.

We say a signature $f$ is \emph{realizable} or \emph{constructible} from a signature set $\mathcal{F}$
if there is a gadget with some dangling edges such that each vertex is assigned a signature from $\mathcal{F}$,
and the resulting graph, when viewed as a black-box signature with inputs on the dangling edges, is exactly $f$.

